in 1961 soviet cosmonaut yuri gagarin  blasted into outer space  and as his vostok rocket blasted off  gagarin shouted cheerfully  that means let's go gagarin's 108  minutes in space was the beginning of  the space age  and an age of space heroes  gagarin was idolized at home and  traveled the world  cheered everywhere by jubilant crowds  his american counterparts  alan shepard followed by john glenn were  similarly lauded as courageous  adventurers  glenn later became a u.s senator and ran  unsuccessfully for president  moscow is go for docking houston is go  for docking  it's up to you guys the space race was a  cold war era strategic competition  between the soviet union and the u.s  with military overtones but it also had  a genuine air of  optimism and idealism seen by many  as the next great stage in the human  saga  and perhaps a force for global  brotherhood  around the world kids dreamed of growing  up to be space  men and women and liftoff of discovery  well things sure have changed from  symbols of national greatness and  demonstrations of dueling ideologies  space travel today is all about money  and ego russia actually started the rich  space  tourist trend offering seats on a soyuz  spacecraft for hefty fees  this july our dream will become a  reality  richard branson never shy of the media  spotlight will swoop space ward  aboard a virgin galactic spacecraft  from a base in new mexico followed by  amazon founder jeff bezos aboard blue  origin a week later  i want to go on this flight because it's  a  thing i've wanted to do all my life  what's really significant about the dmu  mission  the third space billionaire elon musk  says he's interested in taking  one of his spacex rockets to the moon  one day  and after that may be mars musk and  bezos are dueling for contracts to  supply the international space station  and  nasa's lunar lander branson wants to  create a space  tourism industry for well-heeled  travelers willing to pay  several hundred thousand dollars for a  quick jaunt  there is big money to be made that's one  small  somehow it all seems a little less noble  than  in the early days when the apollo  astronauts left behind on the moon  a plaque reading we came in peace for  all mankind  the new space tycoons may not have the  right stuff  but they've got all the bucks rob  reynolds al jazeera
 

